# help reference prints every command as one Markdown page on stdout, and opens no database.
env CORONER_DB=$WORK/absent/coroner.db
exec coroner help reference
cmp stdout reference.md
! stderr .
! exists $WORK/absent

-- reference.md --
# coroner

~~~text
coroner records what happened during an investigation, in order: name a timeline,
add timestamped events from logs, chat and people, then view and export it.
~~~

~~~text
coroner <command> [flags]
~~~

Commands:

~~~text
annotation   Attach evidence and comments to events and timelines
attachment   List, delete and export files attached to events and annotations
completion   Generate the autocompletion script for the specified shell
event        Add, list, view, edit, delete and link events
help         Help about any command or topic
import       Import records from a JSONL export
overlay      Save sets of timelines to read together
source       List sources and fix their names
status       Show the database and the timeline commands act on
tag          List the tags events carry
timeline     Create, list and switch between timelines
version      Show coroner's version
~~~

Help topics:

~~~text
environment  Environment variables coroner reads
exit-codes   Exit codes and the JSON error envelope
formatting   Output formats and what scripts can rely on
time         How coroner reads the times you type
reference    Every command and flag on one Markdown page
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner version --json
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --version   Show coroner's version
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner annotation

~~~text
An annotation is evidence, raw material kept verbatim such as a log line, or a comment,
attached to an event or to the whole timeline. It has no time of its own in the
chronology.
~~~

~~~text
coroner annotation [flags]
coroner annotation <command> [flags]
~~~

Commands:

~~~text
add          Attach evidence or a comment to an event or the timeline
delete       Delete annotations
edit         Change an annotation's body, kind, source or URL, or attach files
list         List a timeline's annotations
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ pbpaste | coroner annotation add - -e 12 --kind evidence --source mongod
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner annotation add

~~~text
Attach an annotation to the event -e names, or to the whole timeline without -e. Evidence
is kept verbatim: pass - to read a log line or chat message from stdin, which loses one
trailing newline and is never read otherwise.

-e takes a seq such as 12, a timeline and seq such as mongo-outage#12, or an event id or
unique id prefix. The event must be in the timeline the annotation goes to.

Each --attach copies a file, such as a screenshot, into the blob directory beside the
database, and prints its id, sha256, size and type.

On a terminal, with no body or --kind, or with --interactive, a form asks for each flag, picks
the event from the timeline, and prints the command each entry equals. Under --json only
--interactive opens it, and --no-input never does.
~~~

~~~text
coroner annotation add {<body> | -} --kind {evidence | comment} [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner annotation add - -e 12 --kind evidence --source mongod < stepdown.log
  $ coroner annotation add "Step-down lands 5 s after the COLLSCAN burst" -e 12 --kind comment
  $ coroner annotation add "Grafana panel at the step-down" -e 12 --kind evidence --attach ./panel.png
  $ coroner annotation add "Root cause: undersized oplog" --kind comment --dry-run --json
  $ coroner annotation add --interactive -e 12
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --attach file        Copy this file in as an attachment; repeat for more
      --author name        Who is adding it, as a name (default: config author, then USER)
      --dry-run            Show the annotation as it would be added, and add nothing
  -e, --event ref          Event to attach it to, as a ref: seq, timeline#seq or id (default: the timeline)
      --interactive        Open a form to fill in, prefilled from the other flags (needs a terminal)
      --kind kind          Annotation kind: {evidence | comment}
      --source source      System, service or person it came from, as a source, created on first use
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on
      --url url            Permalink to the log line or message, as a url
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner annotation delete

~~~text
Delete annotations by full id or unique id prefix, in any timeline, with their attachments
and the blobs no other attachment uses. Every id is checked before anything is deleted, so
an id that matches nothing deletes none. --dry-run shows what would be deleted instead.
~~~

~~~text
coroner annotation delete <id>... [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner annotation delete 0199a1b2-7c1e --dry-run
  $ coroner annotation delete 0199a1b2-7c1e 0199a1b2-7d40 --json
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --dry-run   Show what would be deleted, and delete nothing
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner annotation edit

~~~text
Change an annotation's body, kind, source or URL. Its author, event and timeline never
change. <id> is a full id or a unique id prefix, in any timeline. --body - reads the body
from stdin, which loses one trailing newline. --attach adds a file to the annotation's
attachments; 'coroner attachment delete' removes one.
~~~

~~~text
coroner annotation edit <id> [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner annotation edit 0199a1b2-7c1e --kind comment
  $ coroner annotation edit 0199a1b2-7c1e --attach ./stepdown-trace.pdf
  $ coroner annotation edit 0199a1b2-7c1e --body - --dry-run --json < stepdown.log
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --attach file     Copy this file in as an attachment; repeat for more
      --body body       New body, or - to read it from stdin
      --dry-run         Show the edited annotation, and change nothing
      --kind kind       New annotation kind: {evidence | comment}
      --source source   New source, created on first use
      --url url         New permalink, as a url
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner annotation list

~~~text
List a timeline's annotations: those on the whole timeline first, then those on events in
the order the events happened, oldest first. -e lists one event's. Each row is the id,
kind, author, event and the body's first line; piped, the fields are tab-separated.

Scripts should treat kind as an open set: later versions may add kinds.
~~~

~~~text
coroner annotation list [-e <ref>] [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner annotation list
  $ coroner annotation list -e 12 --json
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
  -e, --event ref          Only the annotations on this event, as a ref: seq, timeline#seq or id
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to act on
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner attachment

~~~text
An attachment is a file, such as a screenshot, kept with one event or annotation. Attach one
with --attach on 'coroner event add', 'coroner event edit', 'coroner annotation add' or
'coroner annotation edit'. Its bytes are stored once, as a blob named by their sha256, in
the directory beside the database.
~~~

~~~text
coroner attachment [flags]
coroner attachment <command> [flags]
~~~

Commands:

~~~text
delete       Delete attachments
export       Write an attachment's file back out
list         List a timeline's attachments
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner event edit 12 --attach ./grafana-p99.png
  $ coroner attachment list -e 12
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

