## coroner import

~~~text
Import the records of a coroner JSONL export, as 'coroner timeline export --format jsonl'
writes it, into the database: its timelines, sources, events, annotations, attachments and
links. Pass - to read the export from stdin, so concatenated exports import together. Every
record is checked before anything is written, and the import is written in one transaction.

Records match by id, and sources by name. A record stored unchanged is skipped, so importing a
file twice changes nothing. A record stored with different content is a conflict: by default
the import writes nothing and exits 4; --on-conflict skip keeps the stored record, and
--on-conflict replace takes the import's. Replace never changes an attachment or link, or moves
an event or annotation, so such a conflict exits 4 under replace too. Authors and created and
updated times come from the export. An imported event keeps its seq unless its timeline has
already reached that seq, when it gets the next one.

An attachment is imported with its file from the attachments/ directory beside the export,
which 'coroner timeline export -o <directory>/' writes, or when the database already has that
file. Otherwise it is left out and listed.

-t names a stored timeline that every record must belong to. Without -t, the export decides
which timelines it imports into.
~~~

~~~text
coroner import {<file> | -} [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner import colleague-annotations.jsonl --dry-run --json
  $ coroner import colleague-annotations.jsonl
  $ cat a.jsonl b.jsonl | coroner import -
  $ coroner import inc-4821.jsonl --on-conflict skip
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --dry-run              Show what the import would change, and write nothing
      --on-conflict policy   What to do with a record stored with different content, as a policy: {fail|skip|replace} (default "fail")
  -t, --timeline name|id     Timeline name|id to act on
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner overlay

~~~text
An overlay is several timelines read together as one chronology. Save one under a name with
'coroner overlay create', then pass --overlay <name> to event list and timeline export instead
of repeating -t. An overlay is kept in this database only: exports never carry it.
~~~

~~~text
coroner overlay [flags]
coroner overlay <command> [flags]
~~~

Commands:

~~~text
create       Save a set of timelines under a name
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner overlay create checkout-incident -t mongo-failover -t checkout-latency
  $ coroner event list --overlay checkout-incident
~~~

