## coroner timeline

~~~text
A timeline is the named chronology of one investigation. Create one per incident or
ticket, then use it so later commands know where events go.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline [flags]
coroner timeline <command> [flags]
~~~

Commands:

~~~text
create       Create a timeline
delete       Delete a timeline and everything in it
edit         Rename a timeline or change its title and refs
export       Export a timeline as JSON, JSONL, Markdown, Mermaid, HTML or SVG
list         List timelines, marking the current one
use          Set the current timeline
view         Show a timeline and its events
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline create mongo-outage-2026-09-11 --ref INC-4821
  $ coroner timeline use mongo-outage-2026-09-11
  $ coroner timeline list
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner timeline create

~~~text
Create a timeline. Its name is how commands select it: at most 64 lowercase letters,
digits, '.', '_' or '-', starting with a letter or digit. Put free text in --title.
Creating a timeline does not make it current; run 'coroner timeline use' for that.

On a terminal, with no name or with --interactive, a form asks for the name, title and
refs, offers the existing timeline when the name is taken, and prints the command it equals.
Under --json only --interactive opens it, and --no-input never does.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline create <name> [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline create mongo-outage-2026-09-11 --title "Primary election storm" \
      --ref INC-4821 --ref https://jira.example.com/browse/INC-4821
  $ coroner timeline create mongo-outage-2026-09-11 --if-not-exists --json
  $ coroner timeline create --interactive
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --dry-run           Show the timeline as it would be created, and create nothing
      --if-not-exists     Print the existing timeline instead of failing when the name is taken
      --interactive       Open a form to fill in, prefilled from the other flags (needs a terminal)
      --ref stringArray   Ticket key or URL the timeline belongs to; repeat for more
      --title title       Free-text title
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner timeline delete

~~~text
Delete a timeline with its events, tags, annotations, attachments and links, and the blobs
no other attachment uses. This cannot be undone, so it needs --yes; --dry-run shows what
would be deleted instead. Deleting the current timeline leaves none current, and the
overlays that read the timeline keep their other timelines. Under --json it prints the
timeline it deletes, or would.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline delete <name|id> {--yes | --dry-run} [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline delete mongo-outage-2026-09-11 --dry-run
  $ coroner timeline delete mongo-outage-2026-09-11 --yes
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --dry-run   Show what would be deleted, and delete nothing
      --yes       Delete without asking
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner timeline edit

~~~text
Change a timeline's name, title or refs. Its id never changes, so its events, and
anything naming it by id, still resolve after a rename. Refs are removed before they are
added; adding a ref it has, or removing one it lacks, changes nothing.

On a terminal, without --name, --title, --add-ref or --remove-ref, or with --interactive, a
form opens on the timeline, or on the current one when none is named, applies only what you
change, and prints the command it equals. Under --json only --interactive opens it, and
--no-input never does.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline edit <name|id> [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline edit mongo-outage-2026-09-11 --name mongo-failover --add-ref INC-4822
  $ coroner timeline edit mongo-failover --title "" --dry-run --json
  $ coroner timeline edit mongo-failover --interactive
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --add-ref stringArray      Add a ticket key or URL; repeat for more
      --dry-run                  Show the edited timeline, and change nothing
      --interactive              Open a form to fill in, prefilled from the other flags (needs a terminal)
      --name name                New name
      --remove-ref stringArray   Remove a ref; repeat for more
      --title title              New title; an empty one clears it
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner timeline export

~~~text
Export the timeline -t, CORONER_TIMELINE or 'coroner timeline use' selects, with its
events, their annotations, the sources they name and their attachments. --format json
writes one document, indented on a terminal; --format jsonl writes a header record, then
one record per line; --format md writes a CommonMark outline for people, with times in
UTC unless --tz names a zone.

--format mermaid writes a Mermaid gantt diagram of the events, one section per source, in
the same zone as md; --diagram appends that diagram to --format md.

--format html writes one self-contained page that shows the events on a timeline in a
browser, in the same zone as md, and needs -o.

--format svg writes a static SVG picture of the events, one lane per source, in the same
zone as md, for documents and slides that draw neither Mermaid nor HTML.

The filter flags mean what they do on event list, and annotations on the events they
leave out are left out too. --order picks which end --limit keeps events from; exported
events are always in the order they happened.

-o writes a file instead of stdout. For md and jsonl, -o may instead name a directory, one
that exists or a path ending in /: coroner writes <timeline>.md or <timeline>.jsonl into
it and each attachment's file under attachments/ beside it, named by its sha256, so the
Markdown's images show wherever the directory is committed or unzipped. Otherwise md links
attachments to the files beside the database, which only this machine has, and says so on
stderr, and a jsonl export's attachment records carry metadata and sha256 only, so their
files stay on this machine. Pasting Markdown into GitHub, Linear or Slack uploads none of
its images.

Repeat -t to overlay several timelines in one json, jsonl, md, mermaid or html export: it
holds every timeline, and their events merge by occurred at, then timeline id, then seq. md
labels each event with its timeline, mermaid draws a section per timeline and source, as in
mongo-failover/mongod, and html nests each timeline's sources under a row for the timeline,
with a checkbox to filter by it. svg exports one timeline. An overlay written into a directory
is named after its timelines joined by +, as in mongo-failover+checkout-latency.jsonl.
--overlay <name> exports the timelines an overlay saved with 'coroner overlay create' holds,
in place of -t. json and jsonl carry every link between two exported events.

--group-by is experimental and may change before 1.0.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline export --format {json | jsonl | md | mermaid | html | svg} [-o {<file> | <directory>/}] [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline export -t mongo-outage --format json > rca.json
  $ coroner timeline export --format jsonl --since "2026-09-11T03:00Z" --source mongod > mongod.jsonl
  $ coroner timeline export --format md --tz America/New_York > rca.md
  $ coroner timeline export --format mermaid --source mongod > timeline.mmd
  $ coroner timeline export --format md --diagram > rca.md
  $ coroner timeline export -t inc-4821 --format md -o rca/
  $ coroner timeline export --format html -o inc-4821.html
  $ coroner timeline export -t inc-4821 --format svg > inc-4821.svg
  $ coroner timeline export -t mongo-failover -t checkout-latency --format jsonl > overlay.jsonl
  $ coroner timeline export --overlay checkout-incident --format md -o rca/
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --contains text      Keep events whose text contains this text, ignoring case
      --diagram            Append a Mermaid gantt diagram of the events (--format md only)
      --group-by field     Section Markdown events by a field: {source|tag} (experimental)
      --include-raw        Add each event's time as it was given (--format md only)
      --limit number       Export at most this number of events (default: every one)
      --order order        Keep the earliest or latest events when --limit cuts the export, as an order: {asc|desc} (default "asc")
  -o, --output path        Write the export to this path, to stdout when it is -, or, for md and jsonl, into a directory with its attachments (default "-")
      --overlay name       Read the timelines of the overlay called name, instead of -t
      --since time         Keep events at or after this time
      --source source      Keep events from this source; repeat to match any of several
      --tag tag            Keep events with this tag; repeat to require all of them
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to export; repeat to overlay several (json, jsonl, md, mermaid and html only)
      --until time         Keep events before this time
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner timeline list

~~~text
List every timeline, oldest first. A '*' marks the current timeline, the one
'coroner timeline use' selected.

On a terminal it opens a picker that also shows each timeline's refs, event count and
last event: / filters, enter makes the timeline under the cursor current, and q quits.
--format text, --json, --no-input, CORONER_NO_INPUT or piping the output prints the list
instead: a marker, the name and the title on each row, tab-separated when piped.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline list [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline list
  $ coroner timeline list --json
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner timeline use

~~~text
Make a timeline current, so commands that act on a timeline use it when neither -t nor
CORONER_TIMELINE names one. Each database keeps its own current timeline. --clear leaves
none current. Under --json it prints the timeline it makes current, or null with --clear.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline use {<name|id> | --clear} [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline use mongo-outage-2026-09-11
  $ coroner timeline use --clear
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --clear     Leave no timeline current
      --dry-run   Show the timeline that would become current, and change nothing
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

## coroner timeline view

~~~text
Show a timeline and its events in the order they happened. Without a name, id or -t,
show the timeline CORONER_TIMELINE or 'coroner timeline use' selects.

On a terminal it opens an interactive viewer, where ? lists the keys and q quits.
Expanding an event shows its attachments: images inline in kitty, Ghostty and iTerm2 3.7 or
later, outside a multiplexer such as tmux and with color on, and elsewhere a placeholder
naming each file, which 'coroner attachment export' copies out.
--format text, piping the output, --no-input or CORONER_NO_INPUT prints plain text instead:
a summary, then the events, each with the gap since the event before it. --source, --tag and
--contains keep the events 'coroner event list' keeps with the same flags; --json prints the
timeline object alone.

Repeat -t to view several timelines as one chronology: their events merge by occurred at, then
timeline id, then seq. The viewer names each event's timeline in a column tinted with that
timeline's color, and plain text prints each timeline's summary and starts each event's row
with its timeline's name. --json prints one timeline object, so it takes one timeline.
~~~

~~~text
coroner timeline view [<name|id>] [flags]
~~~

Examples:

~~~console
  $ coroner timeline view mongo-outage-2026-09-11
  $ coroner timeline view --tag election --source mongod
  $ coroner timeline view -t mongo-failover -t checkout-latency
  $ coroner timeline view --json
~~~

Flags:

~~~text
      --contains text      Keep events whose text contains this text, ignoring case
      --source source      Keep events from this source; repeat to match any of several
      --tag tag            Keep events with this tag; repeat to require all of them
  -t, --timeline name|id   Timeline name|id to view; repeat to view several as one chronology
~~~

Global flags:

~~~text
      --config path     Config file path (env: CORONER_CONFIG)
      --db path         Database file path (env: CORONER_DB)
      --debug           Print debug detail on stderr (env: CORONER_DEBUG)
      --format format   Output format: {text|json|jsonl}, and more on timeline export (default "text")
  -h, --help            Show help for a command
      --json            Short for --format json
      --no-color        Disable color (env: NO_COLOR, CORONER_NO_COLOR)
      --no-input        Never open a form, picker or viewer (env: CORONER_NO_INPUT)
  -q, --quiet           Print only essential output
      --tz zone         Time zone for this invocation (default: config default_timezone, then TZ)
~~~

